course: religion-ethics
lesson: 29
title: Churches of the English-speaking world II
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: More church families: Presbyterians, led by elected elders, with roots in Scotland and John Calvin; Pentecostals, with lively worship and a strong stress on the Holy Spirit; and Quakers, who worship mostly in silence.
explain: The Salvation Army was founded in London by William and Catherine Booth; it is organised like an army, with uniforms and brass bands, and is famous for helping the poor.
explain: Many people today go to non-denominational or free churches, independent churches not part of a bigger family, often with modern songs and bands.
explain: Churches differ in leaders, baptism, style of worship, use of images and how they read the Bible, but all share belief in one God, Jesus who died and rose, the Bible, prayer, baptism and love of neighbour.
explain: Christianity is the largest world religion, and most Christians now live in Africa, Latin America and Asia; ecumenism means churches working together, and for a child it means your friend's church is part of the same big family.
fact: Presbyterian churches are led by elected elders, called presbyters.
fact: Presbyterian churches have roots in Scotland and in the teaching of John Calvin; the Church of Scotland is Presbyterian.
fact: Pentecostal churches stress the Holy Spirit, lively worship, healing and speaking in tongues: praying aloud in sounds or a language the speaker never learned, which they believe shows the Holy Spirit at work today.
fact: Pentecostal churches are named after Pentecost, when the Holy Spirit came on the first disciples.
fact: Quakers, the Religious Society of Friends, worship mostly in silence and are known for peace-making and equality.
fact: The Salvation Army was founded in London by William and Catherine Booth and is famous for brass bands and helping the poor; it wears uniforms because it sees itself as an army fighting poverty and sin.
fact: The Salvation Army's motto of care is often put as soup, soap and salvation.
fact: Non-denominational and free churches are independent churches not part of a larger denomination.
fact: Ecumenism means churches working together and seeking unity, for example in shared services and charities.
fact: Churches differ in leaders, sacraments, baptism, worship style, use of images and how they read the Bible.
fact: All churches share belief in one God, Jesus as Saviour who died and rose, the Bible, prayer, baptism and love of neighbour.
fact: Quakers and the Salvation Army do not usually hold baptism with water, so some list baptism as shared by nearly all churches.
fact: Christianity is the largest world religion, with about a third of the world's people.
fact: Today most Christians live in Africa, Latin America and Asia.
exercise: Who leads a Presbyterian church? | Elected elders | A single bishop | The Pope | A general
exercise: Which national church is Presbyterian? | The Church of Scotland | The Church of England | The Episcopal Church | The Greek Orthodox Church
exercise: Whose teaching gave Presbyterians their roots? | John Calvin | John Wesley | Martin Luther King | William Booth
exercise: What do Pentecostal churches especially stress? | The Holy Spirit and lively worship | Silence and stillness, with nobody speaking at all | Icons and chanted prayers | Bishops and ancient ceremonies
exercise: Why are Pentecostal churches named after Pentecost? | At Pentecost the Holy Spirit came on the disciples | At Pentecost Jesus was born in Bethlehem | At Pentecost the church split East and West | At Pentecost the first church was built
exercise: How do Quakers mostly worship? | In silence | With brass bands | With loud drums | With chanting choirs
exercise: What are Quakers known for? | Peace-making and equality | Army uniforms and marching | Very long sermons | Being led by the Pope
exercise: What is the proper name of the Quakers? | The Religious Society of Friends | The Church of the Brethren | The Fellowship of Saints and Peacemakers | The Order of Peace
exercise: Who founded the Salvation Army? | William and Catherine Booth | John and Charles Wesley | Peter and Andrew | George and Mary Fox
exercise: What is the Salvation Army famous for? | Brass bands and helping the poor | Silent worship and peace-making in meeting houses | Icons, incense and long chanted prayers | Electing elders to lead each congregation
exercise: What is a non-denominational church? | An independent church not part of a larger denomination | A church that belongs to every denomination at once | A church that has no members at all | A church that only opens at Christmas
exercise: What is ecumenism? | Churches working together and seeking unity | One church trying to close the others | Churches competing for the most members | A rule that all churches must become exactly the same
exercise: Which is a real way churches differ? | How they baptise and who leads them | Whether they believe in God at all | Whether Jesus was a real person | Whether loving your neighbour matters
exercise: Which belief do all churches share? | Jesus died and rose again | The Pope leads every church | Worship must be in silence | Only adults can be baptised
exercise: About what share of the world's people are Christians? | About a third | About a tenth | About half | Nearly all
exercise: Where do most Christians live today? | Africa, Latin America and Asia | Only in Europe | Only in the USA, Canada and Australia | Only in the Middle East
test: Emma says Presbyterians are run by the Archbishop of Canterbury. right? | No, they are led by elected elders | Yes, he leads every church in Britain | Yes, but only in Scotland | No, they are led by the Pope
test: My friend went to a Quaker meeting and said "nobody said anything for ages, was it broken?" What would you tell her? | Quakers worship mostly in silence, waiting on God | The minister was ill so the service was cancelled | Quakers are not allowed to speak in public ever | The band forgot their instruments that day
test: A lady in a uniform with a brass band is collecting for the poor outside the shops. Which church is she most likely from? | The Salvation Army | The Quakers | The Orthodox Church | The Presbyterian Church
test: A classmate who is Hindu asks: "if Christians agree, why so many churches?" Best answer? | They share core beliefs but differ in traditions and leaders | They disagree about whether God exists | Each church worships a different God | They only split because of money
test: lol my cousin's church has drums and a light show, is that even a real church | Yes, many free churches use modern worship | No, a real church must have an organ and a choir in robes | No, only Pentecostal churches are ever allowed to use music | Yes, but only if a bishop is present to keep things proper
test: Your older brother says "Christianity is basically a European thing." Fix it. | Most Christians today live in Africa, Latin America and Asia | He is right, most Christians are in Europe | Most Christians today live in North America | Christianity is only found in the Middle East, where it began long ago
test: The churches in your town run one food bank and an Easter walk together. What is this called? | Ecumenism | A schism | A reformation | A denomination
test: A Pentecostal friend says she speaks in tongues at church. What does her church believe it shows? | The Holy Spirit's power today | That she has learned French at school | That she is the leader of the church | That she is not allowed to pray in English
test: Theo says: "Quakers and Baptists don't even believe the same about Jesus." What is more accurate? | They share belief in Jesus who died and rose; their worship differs | Quakers do not believe in Jesus at all | Baptists believe Jesus never rose again | They agree on nothing about Jesus
test: why'd the Salvation Army wear army stuff, they're not soldiers | They saw themselves as an army fighting poverty and sin | They were really part of the British Army and fought in its wars | Uniforms were cheaper than ordinary clothes | The King ordered all churches to wear them
test: The judge asks: "Does unity mean all churches must become the same?" | No, they can work together and still keep different traditions | Yes, unity means every church must close but one | Yes, all churches must use the same building | No, unity means churches should never meet
exam: Identify the form of church governance whereby leadership is exercised by elected elders. | Presbyterian | Episcopal | Papal | Congregational
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the worship of the Religious Society of Friends. | It is conducted principally in silence | It is conducted principally with brass bands | It is conducted principally in chant before icons | It is conducted principally with drums and lights
exam: Ascertain the regions wherein the majority of the world's Christians presently reside. | Africa, Latin America and Asia | Western Europe exclusively | North America exclusively | The Middle East exclusively
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises ecumenism? | The co-operation of churches in pursuit of unity | The division of one church into many rival churches | The founding of independent congregations apart from others | The appointment of bishops over elders in every church
